                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h1] Game Summary: [/h1] \nThe simple way to summarize this game is that it's a classic arcade space shooter, to the extent your objective is to obtain a high score.  Doing so is as simple as building up a point multiplier by killing enemies and staying alive.  Unfortunately, there's nothing more to the game than that.  Except for an option to play the game where the screen is almost entirely black, with only a small radius visible around your ship, there are no other game modes or features to play around with.  I suppose this dark mode option does grant some novelty factor, but it doesn't show much versatility or creativity on the developers' part.  It makes me disappointed that their innovation stopped there, as they could have expanded with a swath of different options and features that could be turned on or off to change up the gameplay and difficulty.  That could have come off as gimmicky, but since it'd all be optional, it wouldn't hurt the game to have more variance.\n\n[h1] Pros: [/h1] \n•\tYou have a choice of three ships to play as, which do have different stats and play differently.  One is completely average, one is fast but weak, and the other is powerful but slow.  \n•\tYour bullets destroy enemy projectiles, but conversely theirs destroy yours too.  Since enemies easily outnumber you, this can quickly work to your disadvantage.  \n•\tYou have three lives per attempt, so even if you die early, you can still achieve a high score with the other lives available.  \n•\tVarious power-ups spawn in the game, such as a more powerful attack and more hit points.  \n•\tThere's only one track in the game, but you can mute it in the options easily enough.  \n\n\n[h1] Cons: [/h1] \n•\tThere's a pink wall surrounding the screen (it doesn't harm you to bump into it), which limits how much room you have to move around to dodge enemies.  It's not very large either, making it seem as if you're playing in a small arena, despite being a spaceship flying around freely in outer space.  Considering the simplicity of the graphics and game, it's annoying to be so constrained.  \n•\tAfter playing only a few rounds of the game, whether with dark mode on or off, you've seen everything the game has to offer.  There's no unlockables, upgrades, boss fights, or other stages to go to.  \n•\tAlthough you do have a hit point system, some attacks, like missiles, are instant kills no many how many hearts you have.  This includes colliding with an enemy ship.  \n\n\n[h1] Conclusion: [/h1] \nThe main issue with this game is that it doesn't do anything to make it stand out from so many similar types of games out there.  Arcade space shooters are oversaturated, and though this game meets the threshold to be functional and playable, it doesn't do anything to be interesting or novel.  Enough effort was put in to make sure it worked properly, so it doesn't come off as a lazy cash-grab, but more could have been done with this basis.  Since the game functions as it should, I wouldn't feel right to give it a thumbs down, so although I recommend this game, I wouldn't suggest anybody go out of their way to get it either.  If it comes your way vie a bundle, I would say it's worth keeping and playing, but you'll likely forget about it after you're done with it.",
